9. API AND SECRET PROTECTION
The goal is to protect private credentials and sensitive implementation details, not to falsely claim that public URLs can be made impossible to discover.
9.1 Audit all existing code
Search the repository, Git history where authorized, build artifacts, APK/AAB files, frontend bundles, source maps, environment files, deployment settings, and logs for:
•	API keys.
•	API secrets.
•	OAuth client secrets.
•	Database connection strings.
•	Database passwords.
•	Signing keys and keystores.
•	Private access tokens.
•	Admin credentials.
•	Private service URLs.
•	Debug endpoints.
•	Unprotected database access.
•	Credentials accidentally included in sample configuration files.
•	Secrets embedded in Android resources or JavaScript bundles.
Never print actual secrets into a report, terminal output, generated prompt, or user interface. Redact sensitive values.
9.2 Correct architecture
Move privileged API calls to a trusted backend or server-side function.
Keep provider credentials in protected server environment variables or an appropriate secret manager. Never put private secrets into:
•	Android source code.
•	APK/AAB assets.
•	Public JavaScript.
•	Browser storage.
•	Public environment variables.
•	Git repositories.
•	Public configuration files.
•	Client-side source maps.
•	Public crash logs.
The mobile app should call the application's own authenticated backend for protected operations. The backend should call the third-party provider using its server-side credentials.
Use separate credentials for development, testing, and production.
Use least-privilege database accounts and restrict each credential to the minimum required operations.
9.3 API endpoints and configuration
Hide private provider endpoints and internal configuration from ordinary user-facing screens. Do not show API keys, tokens, database URLs, raw environment variables, stack traces, or internal server configuration in the app.
However, do not rely on hiding an endpoint URL as a security control. Public client requests can be inspected.
Protect backend endpoints with:
•	Authentication where required.
•	Authorization for every protected resource.
•	Server-side input validation.
•	Rate limiting and abuse controls.
•	Request size limits.
•	Safe error messages.
•	Secure database queries.
•	Correct CORS configuration for a web client.
•	CSRF protection where the authentication design requires it.
•	Secure session handling.
•	Password hashing with an appropriate modern algorithm.
•	Audit logging that does not record secrets.
•	Request timeouts and safe retry behavior.
Never place a third-party private key in a request sent directly from the app.
Use a provider allowlist and restrict which upstream hosts the backend can contact. Do not create a generic open proxy.
9.4 Rotate exposed secrets
If a private secret has been committed, included in a public build, or exposed in logs:
1.	Identify the affected credential without displaying it.
2.	Revoke or rotate it at the provider.
3.	Update the server's secret configuration.
4.	Check relevant access logs.
5.	Remove it from the current source and build artifacts.
6.	Address Git history exposure according to repository policy.
7.	Rebuild and test the app.
8.	Verify that the old credential no longer works.
Removing a secret from source code alone does not make an already exposed credential safe.
